        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:  <BR>The central hypothesis is that dose-rate effects in the 1 to 0.005 Gy/min range will be the same for protons vs. gamma-rays for chromosomal aberration induction. Second, levels of chromosomal radiosensitivity, and their dose-rate dependence will be similar for cells from individuals with genetic differences in radiosensitivity. Such a biomarker may help identify differences among individuals in a component contributing to their susceptibility to these effects. 
<P>
APPROACH:  <BR>The approach for this investigation is unavailable.
<P>
RESULTS:  <BR>For low passage normal human fibroblasts and for MCF10A human mammary epithelial cells, the dose rate effect for induction of chromosomal aberrations seen for gamma-rays in the range of 70cGy/min (HDR) to 0.005cGy/min (LDR) was virtually identical for protons with energies of 1000MeV or 50MeV and corresponded to a dose rate reduction factor(DREF) of about 2.0 for all these radiations. There was a smaller, but still a significant dose rate effect for 1GeV/n Fe ions corresponding to a DREF of about 1.3 despite the fact that the LET for the Fe ions was approximately 150 keV/micrometer. For the MCF10A cells, the responses (aberration induction, cell killing and gamma H2AX formation at low dose rates) in 2D or 3D culture were identical. Indistinguishable dose rate effects were also seen for ATM +/- cells compared to ATM +/+ cells with the two cell strains that were derived from individuals who were siblings from the same family. 
<P>
        </div>

